  "subject": "💰 Axios PM: Biggest IPO ever",
  "date": "Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:10:39 -0500",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🎓 Plus: GTA class | Thursday, November 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios PM \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen &middot; Nov 19, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Good Thursday afternoon. Today's newsletter, edited by Alex Fitzpatrick, is 538 words, a 2-min. read. Thanks to Amy Stern for copy editing . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Anthropic IPO spikes, then settles \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Samyukta Lakshmi/Bloomberg via Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n AI giant Anthropic opened for trading today in the biggest public stock offering ever, despite investor fears of a possible AI bubble and anxiety that the technology threatens lives and livelihoods.\n\n Anthropic shares began trading at $111.50, spiking more than 30% above the initial public offering price. They fell to around $98 as of midafternoon. 💰 A day earlier, the company priced its IPO at $83 per share. \n\n That was enough to value the entire business at $1.5 trillion, surpassing SpaceX as the largest IPO on record. At the start of trading, Anthropic's valuation leaped to $2.01 trillion. Between the lines: The initial price was surprisingly high given investor worries since the stunning ouster of AI's chief promoter, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, amid a possible Chinese spying scandal.\n\n 📈 What we're watching: What happens to the share price before today's 4 p.m. ET close.\n\n Go deeper ... More from Dan Primack . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🤖 Bots fueled outage conspiracies \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios Bot accounts helped spread conspiracy theories about the recent major tech outages, Axios' Sam Sabin reports from exclusive new research. \n\n 🖼️ The big picture: Purveyors of disinformation appear to be exploiting public anxiety over the outages and fragile infrastructure, amplifying suspicions of coordinated cyberattacks and government cover-ups.\n\n It's unclear who's behind these accounts. But the pattern shows that when technology failures lack substantive explanations, bot networks will fill the void to manufacture panic. By the numbers: Suspected bots accounted for roughly 30% of posts promoting conspiracy theories about several recent tech outages, according to data from social media monitoring firm PeakMetrics.\n\n During Microsoft's outage, bots accounted for 26% of conspiratorial posts. 🤳 Bots accounted for 34% of conspiracy-related posts during Friday's Workday outage, compared with 27% of other posts.\n\n Likely bot accounts also made up 31% of conspiracy-related posts about Bank of America's outage, and 28% of posts linking Chase's outage to other disruptions. Go deeper . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 3. ⚡️ Catch me up \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n President Trump, Chinese President Xi Jinping and Russian President Vladimir Putin meet today in Shenzhen, China. Photo: Kremlin Press Service/Anadolu via Getty Images 🇺🇸 🇷🇺 🇨🇳 The U.S., Russia and China emerged from their historic trilateral summit with a declaration on nuclear risks and energy security, but no major diplomatic breakthroughs. Go deeper . 🇲🇲 In a landmark judgment, the United Nations' top court ruled that Myanmar committed genocide against the Rohingya minority in a brutal 2017 military operation. Go deeper . 🛰️ NASA's Voyager 1 probe has phoned home after crossing one light-day from Earth. It and its cousin, Voyager 2, are humanity's farthest-flung spacecraft. Go deeper . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 4. 👨‍🏫 1 for the road: The GTA professor \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios University of Tennessee history professor Tore Olsson is using the hit gaming franchise Grand Theft Auto to dive into bigger questions about modern America, Axios Nashville's Adam Tamburin reports. \n\n 🎮 Olsson's \"Grand Theft America\" course isn't the gamer gab session some students might expect.\n\n He estimates that no more than 10% of class time is devoted to discussing fictional events from the games. The rest delves into globalization, mass incarceration, the war on drugs and other forces that have shaped America since 1980. 🤯 Olsson tells Axios: \"I'm getting them in the door with pop culture ... and then convincing them to care, and then trying to blow their minds with content and approaches and perspectives that they've never considered previously.\"\n\n Go deeper ... Get Axios Local . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 📬 Thanks for reading! Please invite your friends to join PM . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
